          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CDCP83RR002",
    "text": "`name` holds the address — either `<block-slug-path>` or `id/<ID>`, the same literal-prefix convention as the link fragment. `<block-slug-path>` is the `/`-joined `slugify()` output of every segment from the document root down to the target; each segment is `slugify()` applied to the block's own raw title text — a heading's raw line (`#`/`##` markers included), or, at unbounded depth in a list, a colon-delimited lead-in term (see [Issues](../issues/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV5A800A)). Hand-written by the author pre-ingestion; projecting the block back to the file mirror adds the `id/<ID>` form alongside it (see [Workflows](#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4TG007)).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDC0D59000Y",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0D590010",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDCP83RR003",
    "text": "`id` is left unassigned by this design — `name` is for anchor/fragment-navigation, `id` is for the (future) webapp's own HTML element management, a different concern this doc doesn't specify. It's also why the slug-path form couldn't go there regardless: `id` values shouldn't contain `/`, and a slug-path does.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDC0D59000Y",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0D590011",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0D590011:links:Link:00CDC0D8FG000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CDBYV4TG007",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0D590011:links:Link:00CDC0D8FG000:props:StringProp:00CDC0D8FG001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "112"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CDC0RAXR011",
    "text": "For a heading, the anchor is stripped out of the raw line before it's used as `title`, and kept separately (see [Workflows](#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4TG007)) — `title` itself stays clean. For a list item or paragraph, nothing is stripped: `text` keeps the anchor exactly where it was written (right after the lead-in colon), unmodified — the block's `title` is a separately-extracted, read-only label taken from the span before the colon, never subtracted out of `text`. That's safe specifically because a list item/paragraph's `text` is what gets rendered back to markdown; `title` isn't independently serialized for these the way it is for a heading, so there's nothing to reassemble.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBYV4T800H",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0D590013",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0D590013:links:Link:00CDC0D8FR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CDBZ7698003",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0D590013:links:Link:00CDC0D8FR000:props:StringProp:00CDC0D8FR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "205"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0D590013:props:StringProp:00CDC0D86G000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0D590013:props:StringProp:00CDC0D86G001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CDC0RAXR013",
    "text": "Two further checks, best-effort rather than exact (a wrongly-detected term is corrected the same way any title now is — edit the lead-in term itself in the text; there's no separate override any more, see [History](../history/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBZ7698003)):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBYV4TG000",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CDC0D598000",
      "BlockNode:00CDC0D598001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0D598000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Length cap**",
    "text": "**Length cap**: <a name='id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4TG002' class='aperas-anchor aperas-id'></a> the visible (non-code) text before the colon must be short — at most 10 words for a space-delimited script, or 20 characters for Japanese (no spaces to count words by). A lead-in term is always short in every real example; a colon only reachable after a long run of ordinary prose is a real English sentence's own internal colon, not a lead-in delimiter — confirmed live that sentence-boundary detection alone doesn't discriminate that case, only length does.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDC0D590013",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0D598001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Space after the colon** (space-delimited scripts only)",
    "text": "**Space after the colon** (space-delimited scripts only): <a name='id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4TG003' class='aperas-anchor aperas-id'></a> a genuine lead-in always reads \"Term: rest\", space included, whereas a technical string that simply never got wrapped in backticks (a bare `aperas://tree/...` URL, a `key:value` pair) almost never has a space right after its own colon. This catches what the inline-code exclusion above can't: a colon that's real data, just not properly fenced as such. Nothing following the colon at all — its own inline content simply ends there, e.g. a lead-in immediately followed only by nested list children, no inline \"rest\" on the same line — counts the same as a space: there's no adjacent non-space character to be suspicious of.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDC0D590013",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0D598006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "At ingestion",
    "text": "At ingestion: <a name='id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4TG008' class='aperas-anchor aperas-id'></a> the target's node id is minted, in the graph only — nothing is written to disk yet. Projecting that block back to the file mirror is what adds an `<a name=\"id/<ID>\" class=\"aperas-anchor aperas-id\">` anchor alongside the block's existing slug-path anchor: appended at line end for a heading; spliced into `text` right after the lead-in colon for a list item or paragraph, leaving the rest of `text` untouched, and only if `text` doesn't already contain that block's own id-anchor (idempotent — re-projecting an already-anchored block doesn't duplicate it). Existing referring links keep resolving against the slug-path anchor unchanged; nothing needs to find or rewrite them. The anchor's `id` attribute itself is not written by this; it's left for the webapp's own future use.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBYV4TG007",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0D598009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0D598009:links:Link:00CDC0D8G0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CDBZ76TR008",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0D598009:links:Link:00CDC0D8G0000:props:StringProp:00CDC0D8G0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "1119"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0D598009:links:Link:00CDC0D8G0002",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CDBYV4TG005",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0D598009:links:Link:00CDC0D8G0002:props:StringProp:00CDC0D8G8000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "1460"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CDC0RAY0003",
    "text": "A referring artifact's own text not changing is not the same as its links staying resolved: the target of a link is free to change shape (a rename, a newly-created anchor, an artifact ingested for the first time) without the referrer's text moving at all, and an artifact whose file hash is unchanged since its last ingestion is never re-ingested on its own — nothing would otherwise re-run its link resolution against the graph's current state. `kg:ingest` closes this gap with a small retry mechanism: every code a block fails to resolve is stashed on its own artifact (one `danglingRef` prop per distinct code, rewritten wholesale — not appended to — on every ingestion, so a code that stops applying disappears with it); after the explicitly-requested artifacts and the folder tree are both settled, every live artifact's stashed codes are retried against the current graph, and any artifact with at least one now-resolvable code is force-reingested — its own text is unchanged, so this is a link-resolution refresh, not a real re-parse, but it goes through the same reconciliation path as any other ingestion (see [Discussion](../discussion/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBZ76TR008) for why re-ingesting isn't the same as re-parsing here, and why one retry pass rather than a fixed-point loop). This applies uniformly to every code form, including a `#fragment` link that never got flagged as dangling in the first place (most never resolve, by design — see [Anchor-Matching Requirement for Resolution](#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4TG005)): it's retried silently either way, just never warned about.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBYV4TG007",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0D8P0009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDC0RDB8003",
    "text": "```markdown\n  <a name='---sub-1/----sub-1-1'></a>\n  ### Sub 1.1\n  \n  [Sub 1.1](#---sub-1/----sub-1-1)\n  ```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBZ7698008",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0D8P000A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0D8P000A:links:Link:00CDC0D9K8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CDBZ76TG002",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0D8P000A:links:Link:00CDC0D9K8000:props:StringProp:00CDC0D9K8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "61"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CDC0RDB8004",
    "text": "Recorded as its own design concern (`design/linking.md`, see [Landing on standard links + injected anchors](../discussion/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBZ76TG002)) rather than folded into `documentation`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBZ7698008",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0DA68009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "The first instinct was a syntactic heuristic",
    "text": "The first instinct was a syntactic heuristic: <a name='id/BlockNode:00CDBZ76TG007' class='aperas-anchor aperas-id'></a> a heading-derived slug always starts with at least two literal dashes (`slugify` runs on the raw `\"## \"` line, so the `#` markers survive as dashes), which an ordinary anchor essentially never does. But that signal doesn't exist for a list-item-derived lead-in term — `slugify(\"Term\")` is just `\"term\"`, indistinguishable in shape from a plain anchor like `#installation`. Narrowing the pattern further (requiring a `/`, say) would have quietly dropped support for single-segment list-item slugs rather than actually resolving the ambiguity.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBZ76TG006",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0DA6800C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "The first fix was mechanical and uncontroversial",
    "text": "The first fix was mechanical and uncontroversial: <a name='id/BlockNode:00CDBZ76TR001' class='aperas-anchor aperas-id'></a> stop scanning raw characters and walk the block's actual parsed inline nodes instead, so `inlineCode` is structurally invisible (mirrors `collectLinkCodes`'s own inlineCode skip) and `strong`/`emphasis` are excluded by real node type rather than by counting `**` pairs.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBZ76TR000",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0DA80001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0DA80001:links:Link:00CDC0DBRR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CDBZ76TR006",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0DA80001:links:Link:00CDC0DBRR000:props:StringProp:00CDC0DBRR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "40"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CDC0RE5R00H",
    "text": "Best-effort throughout, not exact — see [Manual titles turned out to be the wrong fix](#id/BlockNode:00CDBZ76TR006) for why a wrongly-detected title is corrected by editing the lead-in term itself, not by a separate override.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBZ76TR000",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0DA80003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0DA80003:links:Link:00CDC0DBS0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CDBZ7698003",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0DA80003:links:Link:00CDC0DBS0000:props:StringProp:00CDC0DBS0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "884"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CDC0RE5R00K",
    "text": "Once lead-in-term extraction existed as a deterministic, always-available function of a block's own text, `kg:title`'s fragility looked less like a feature with a rough edge and more like a whole mechanism worth dropping: an out-of-band override that only worked as long as you never touched the text around it wasn't actually more practical than editing the text itself. `kg:title` (and `carryForwardFields`'s title-carrying line) was removed outright — a title is now always a pure function of the current text, recomputed on every parse, for every block type. `kg:update` gained a compensating capability for the one block type that actually needed it: a heading's title is projected back to disk (no other block type's is), so `kg:update` now treats a piped leading heading line as the target's full new state, title included, instead of only ever touching `text`/`children` (see [History](../history/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBZ7698003)).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBZ76TR006",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0DA80005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "The first fix considered was the blunt one",
    "text": "The first fix considered was the blunt one: <a name='id/BlockNode:00CDBZ76TR009' class='aperas-anchor aperas-id'></a> always re-ingest everything, dropping the hash check entirely. Rejected immediately — it throws away the entire reason the check exists (a large corpus re-parsed on every single `kg:ingest` call, most of it for nothing) to fix a problem that only actually affects a small, identifiable set of artifacts: the ones with an unresolved reference in the first place. A targeted retry needs to know which artifacts those are without re-scanning everything — so a dangling code, instead of being logged and forgotten (the pre-existing behavior), is stashed on its own referring artifact as a `danglingRef` prop, reusing the existing generic props mechanism rather than inventing new storage. After every `kg:ingest` run's main work is done, every artifact with at least one stashed code gets that code retried against the *current* graph; a match forces exactly that one artifact through link resolution again.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBZ76TR008",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0DA80007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "One retry pass, not a loop to a fixed point",
    "text": "One retry pass, not a loop to a fixed point: <a name='id/BlockNode:00CDBZ76TR00B' class='aperas-anchor aperas-id'></a> artifact A's own retry unblocking artifact B, whose retry then unblocks artifact C, is possible in principle but not a real pattern this corpus (or a documentation corpus in general) produces — a single sweep after the main ingestion work catches every case actually seen, and a longer chain can be revisited if one ever shows up in practice.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBZ76TR008",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0DA8000B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0DA8000B:links:Link:00CDC0DBS8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CDBZ76TR006",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0DA8000B:links:Link:00CDC0DBS8000:props:StringProp:00CDC0DBS8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "825"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "The fix",
    "text": "The fix: <a name='id/BlockNode:00CDBZ76TR00F' class='aperas-anchor aperas-id'></a> treat \"nothing left in this text run\" as satisfying the space-after-colon check, the same as an actual space. This doesn't touch the two guards that already run first and stay exactly as strict as before — a colon inside `inlineCode`/`code` is still never a candidate at all, and a colon inside `strong`/`emphasis` still never qualifies — only a colon that's already passed both of those is affected. Accepted as a known, deliberate side effect: a colon immediately followed by a *different* inline node with no space (`` note:**bold** ``) now also passes, since that colon's own text run ends the same way a genuinely-safe one does. Not treated as a real regression — matches the feature's existing best-effort, user-correctable philosophy ([Manual titles turned out to be the wrong fix](#id/BlockNode:00CDBZ76TR006)) rather than staying maximally strict at the cost of a real, common case — a lead-in immediately followed by nested children, the exact shape this concern's own docs use throughout — going untitled.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBZ76TR00E",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0DC38005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0DC38005:links:Link:00CDC0DDBG000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CDBZ76TG003",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0DC38005:links:Link:00CDC0DDBG000:props:StringProp:00CDC0DDBG001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "186"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CDC0RFD8005",
    "text": "No anchor *recognition/absorption* mechanism is needed for Slice 1 — a hand-written tree anchor is left as ordinary content and round-trips today with zero new code, confirmed live (see [Dropping anchor recognition entirely](../discussion/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBZ76TG003)). The only new mechanism is emitting a fresh id-anchor at projection time.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBZ77EG002",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0DC3G008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0DC3G008:links:Link:00CDC0DDMR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CDBYV5A8003",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CDC0DC3G008:links:Link:00CDC0DDMR000:props:StringProp:00CDC0DDMR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "146"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CDC0RFDG00C",
    "text": "Longer-term, this scratch-ingest check is worth folding into `verify.ts` as a new numbered step — not required to land this slice, but tracked in [issues/linking.md](../issues/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV5A8003) so it isn't lost.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBZ77F8003",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDCMWPC8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDCMWPC8001",
    "text": "[Full-Path Collisions](../design/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4TG006) is a writer-facing authoring rule, not a system-managed lifecycle concept — the system's only job is to detect a violation and refuse it, never to fix it or track history on the writer's behalf.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBZ77F0010",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDCMWPC8002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDCMWPC8002",
    "text": "A collision is the new block's computed slug-path matching another live block's current `toPath()`, *or* any anchor `name` still embedded in that block's `treeAnchor` prop or raw text — [Anchor-Matching Requirement for Resolution](../design/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4TG005) already treats either as a live resolvable target today, so both are real collisions, not just the current title. `astParser.ts`'s `extractAnchorNames` is the new find-all counterpart to `TRAILING_HEADING_ANCHOR_RE` this needed, for the list-item/paragraph case (headings already had a way via `treeAnchor`).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBZ77F0010",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDCMWPC8003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "On collision",
    "text": "On collision: throws, rejecting the whole write (no partial write), naming both colliding blocks/paths. See [A bare snowflake isn't a full node id](../discussion/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBZ76TR00C) for a real bug this surfaced while landing it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBZ77F0010",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDCMWPC8004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDCMWPC8004",
    "text": "`kg:update`'s own check covers only the renamed heading itself, not its separately-reconciled overflow subtree — a freshly-introduced multi-level tree is `kg:insert`'s own check to catch.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBZ77F0010",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDCTB49R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## A multi-artifact kg:ingest isn't transactional",
    "text": "Found live, not reasoned about in the abstract: ingesting `design/linking.md issues/linking.md history/linking.md discussion/linking.md planning/linking.md` in one `kg:ingest` call failed on the third path (`history/linking.md`'s duplicate-date collision), and the CLI reported the whole call as failed — but `design/linking.md` and `issues/linking.md`, processed earlier in the same loop, had already been fully ingested into the live in-memory `Store` by the time the failure surfaced. `ingestArtifacts`/`ingestAllArtifacts` (`apeironNgn/artifacts.ts`) loop over paths calling `ingestArtifact` per file with no `try`/`catch` around each iteration; nothing rolls back what an earlier iteration already committed when a later one throws. This is a lack of transaction semantics, not a one-off bug — the same shape of gap would apply to any batched multi-step write, `kg:insert`/`kg:update` sequences included, not just `kg:ingest`.",
    "parent": "ArtifactNode:00CDBYV46R000",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CDCTB4A0000",
      "BlockNode:00CDCTB4A0001",
      "BlockNode:00CDCTB4A0002"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDCTB4A0000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "The failure was only discovered indirectly",
    "text": "The failure was only discovered indirectly: a *later*, corrected re-run reported \"Tracked 1, skipped 4 unchanged\" instead of \"Tracked 5\" — the only sign that two of the five files had already been silently, partially processed by the earlier failed attempt. The pending `--flush` for that failed call never ran, so none of that partial progress reached disk that time — but it existed in the service's memory regardless, invisible until a later command exposed it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDCTB49R001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDCTB4A0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDCTB4A0001",
    "text": "The real risk, specifically for the graph-first editing direction (edit via `kg:insert`/`kg:update`, then `kg:project`): a scripted multi-step sequence that fails partway leaves the live graph ahead of disk in a way that's easy to miss, and a service restart before noticing would silently discard that in-memory-only progress with no error at all. Worth treating \"the command reported failure\" as \"go check what state things are actually in,\" not \"nothing happened.\"",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDCTB49R001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDCTB4A0002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDCTB4A0002",
    "text": "**Status: identified, not yet implemented.** No fix landed this pass — recorded here first, per the deliberate policy going forward to document a gap like this before touching code. A real fix would need `ingestArtifacts`/`ingestAllArtifacts` to either stop after the first failure and report exactly which paths already committed (least invasive), or gain real transactional semantics across a whole batch (bigger, would need every mutation this session has relied on — `hydrateFromParsed`, `applyTombstone`, `resolveBlockLinks` — to be stageable and revocable, not just applied directly).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDCTB49R001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDD68DZG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDEYP37G001",
    "text": "Working reports separate from the design docs one level up — currently just `bugs/`, filed while\ninvestigating unexpected behavior in the live system.",
    "parent": "FolderNode:00CDD68DZ8000",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDD68E00001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDEYP380001",
    "text": "Open bug reports. Each is filed once a real, reproduced defect is found in the live ApeironNgn\nsubstrate; once fixed and verified, its report moves to `resolved/` rather than being deleted, so\nthe investigation stays around as a record. No open reports at the moment.",
    "parent": "FolderNode:00CDD68DZR000",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDD68E08001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDEYP388001",
    "text": "Closed bug reports, moved here from `bugs/` once the underlying defect was fixed and the fix was\nverified live — kept as a record of what broke and why, not left in the open-bugs folder above.",
    "parent": "FolderNode:00CDBYV4CG002",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDD68E80001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "# Architecture of Documents: Separation of Concerns & Matrix Workflow",
    "parent": "ArtifactNode:00CDD68DS8000",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CDD68E80002",
      "BlockNode:00CDD68E80007",
      "BlockNode:00CDD68E8000E",
      "BlockNode:00CDD68E88002"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDD68E80002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Context",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDD68E80001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CDD68E80003"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDD68E80003",
